1 => (n,1)\n\nNow diagonal x+y=n covers points with sum n: (1,n-1),(2,n-2),...,(n-1,1). Among the remaining columns, which points are on this diagonal? For a=n-3, b = n - (n-3) = 3 => (n-3,3). For a=n-2, b = n - (n-2) = 2 => (n-2,2). For a=n-1, b = n - (n-1) = 1 => (n-1,1). So diagonal x+y=n covers (n-3,3),(n-2,2),(n-1,1). Good.\n\nDiagonal x+y=n+1 covers points with sum n+1: (1,n),(2,n-1),...,(n,n+1-n) = (n,1). Among remaining columns: a=n-3 => b = n+1-(n-3) = 4 => (n-3,4). a=n-2 => b = n+1-(n-2) = 3 => (n-2,3). a=n-1 => b = n+1-(n-1) = 2 => (n-1,2). a=n => b = n+1-n = 1 => (n,1). So diagonal x+y=n+1 covers (n-3,4),(n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1).\n\nThus after using these two diagonals, the points covered among the remaining columns are:\n\n- (n-3,3) covered by x+y=n.\n- (n-3,4) covered by x+y=n+1.\n- (n-2,2) covered by x+y=n.\n- (n-2,3) covered by x+y=n+1.\n- (n-1,1) covered by x+y=n.\n- (n-1,2) covered by x+y=n+1.\n- (n,1) covered by x+y=n+1.\n\nThus uncovered points among the remaining columns are:\n\n- (n-3,1),(n-3,2)\n- (n-2,1)\n- (n-2,? Actually (n-2,1) is uncovered.\n- (n-1,? (n-1,1) covered, (n-1,2) covered)\n- (n,? (n,1) covered)\n\nThus uncovered points: (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-2,1). That's 3 points. We have 2 sunny lines left to cover them. As before, any pair among these three points has forbidden slope: (n-3,1)-(n-2,1) horizontal, (n-3,1)-(n-3,2) vertical, (n-3,2)-(n-2,1) slope = (2-1)/(n-3-(n-2)) = 1/(-1) = -1. So cannot cover any pair with a sunny line. So we need at least 3 lines to cover them, but we have only 2 sunny lines. So this construction fails.\n\nThus the pattern suggests that k=2 is impossible for n≥5? But we found a construction for n=5 using vertical lines x=1,2,3 and two sunny lines covering (4,1) and (5,1). That works because the leftover points after vertical lines x=1,2,3 are (4,1),(5,1) only, which can be covered by two sunny lines individually. So for n=5, k=2 works. For n=4, k=2 works (vertical lines x=1,2, leftover (3,1),(3,2) can be covered 